# HMD-0034: Mermaid, drawn by a native renderer

**Status**: accepted
**Created**: 2026-11-25
**Source**: [the board card that opened the work](../../issues/0115-mermaid-diagrams-on-par-with-d2.hmd)

## Abstract

Mermaid is a diagram language of HyperMarkDown with the same standing as D2.
A fence whose info string opens `mermaid#show` is a diagram cell, and every
tool that draws a `d2#show` cell draws it. The renderer is `merman-cli`, a
native program that draws Mermaid without a browser. It is run the way `d2` is
run: the fence body on standard input, SVG on standard output, within the same
bounds, delivered as a `data:` image. It is pinned in the toolchain beside
`d2`. This record reverses the earlier rejection of Mermaid as superseded by
D2. Neither language is the default.

## Motivation

- **Readers already know Mermaid.** GitHub, GitLab, and Obsidian draw it, and
  it is the diagram language most Markdown authors have used. A card brought in
  from elsewhere carries Mermaid, not D2.
- **HQL already writes Mermaid.** Its `mermaid` format step emits a Mermaid
  fence, and every preview and site build then shows that output as code. One
  half of the toolchain produces a diagram that the other half does not draw.
- **The reason for the rejection was the renderer.** Mermaid's reference
  renderer is a JavaScript library that lays out text through a browser, and
  its command-line tool drives a headless Chromium. That is hundreds of
  megabytes per platform and a cold start of seconds. A native renderer removes
  both costs, so the rejection no longer has a reason.

**What this must not break.** A card with Mermaid cells renders completely
with no renderer installed: each cell shows its source and says why it is not
drawn. D2 keeps everything it has.

## Goals

- There is nothing D2 gets that Mermaid does not: the fence rule, the
  TypeScript implementation, the site build, the editor preview, the toolchain
  pin, the bounds, the image delivery, and the degraded state.
- One renderer draws Mermaid in the editor and on the site, so a diagram looks
  the same in both.
- No browser, no Node runtime, and no container is needed to draw a diagram.

## Non-goals

- **Drawing a plain `mermaid` fence.** A plain `mermaid` fence is a listing,
  shown as code. This is the rule for every diagram language, and it means a
  Mermaid block written for GitHub or Obsidian shows as code here until its
  fence is renamed to `mermaid#show`.
- **Interactive diagrams.** A drawing reaches the page as an image, so a
  `click` directive, a link, or a tooltip inside it does nothing.
- **Pixel parity with mermaid.js.** The renderer follows Mermaid's syntax and
  layout closely. It is a second implementation, and a drawing can differ in
  detail from what a browser draws.
- **A second renderer as a fallback.** A diagram drawn by one renderer in the
  editor and another on the site would differ from itself.

## Specification

### The fence

- A fence whose kind is `mermaid#show` MUST be a diagram cell. Matching is
  exact and case-sensitive: `Mermaid#show` is a listing.
- A plain `mermaid` fence MUST NOT be drawn.
- The body of a Mermaid cell is opaque to the resolver. It yields no link, no
  embed, no anchor, and no graph edge, even where Mermaid's own syntax uses
  double square brackets for a node shape.

````markdown
```mermaid#show
flowchart LR
  client --> server
```

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  client --> server
```
````

The first is drawn; the second is shown as code.

### The renderer

The renderer is `merman-cli`, from the `merman` project, version 0.11.0.

- It is a native program written in Rust. It starts no browser and needs no
  JavaScript runtime.
- It publishes a binary for each of the five toolchain platforms: Linux on
  x86-64 and ARM64, macOS on x86-64 and ARM64, and Windows on x86-64.
- Its licence is MIT or Apache-2.0, at the user's choice. The layout engine it
  bundles for some diagram types is under EPL-2.0. The toolchain archive
  carries the licence files with the binary.
- It follows Mermaid 12 and draws more than thirty diagram types, including
  flowchart, sequence, class, state, entity-relationship, Gantt, pie, mindmap,
  timeline, and Git graph.

An implementation MUST run it with exactly these arguments, with the fence
body on standard input:

```text
merman-cli render - --format svg --svg-pipeline resvg-safe \
    --resource-profile constrained --operation-timeout-ms 2000 --quiet
```

- `render -` reads the source from standard input and writes the drawing to
  standard output. No path, no workspace, and no environment from the card
  reaches the process.
- `--svg-pipeline resvg-safe` draws every label as SVG text. Mermaid's default
  draws labels as HTML inside a `foreignObject` element, and an image does not
  show the content of that element: the diagram would arrive with empty boxes.
- `--resource-profile constrained` is the renderer's own resource policy for
  input nobody has vetted. It bounds the source size, the size of the parsed
  model, the layout work, and the size of the SVG.
- `--operation-timeout-ms 2000` is the renderer's own deadline. The
  implementation still kills the process at the shared bound.

The renderer MUST NOT be given `--allow-network`. Without it the renderer
opens no connection, so an icon pack named in a diagram is not fetched.

### Bounds

Mermaid shares the numbers D2 has. Both implementations refuse the same input
for the same stated reason.

| Bound | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Source size | 64 KiB |
| Time for one render | 2 seconds |
| Cached drawings | 64, least recently used evicted |

The 2-second bound holds with room to spare. On an Apple M-series machine a
flowchart, a sequence diagram, a class diagram, a state diagram, an
entity-relationship diagram, and a Gantt chart each drew in about 10
milliseconds, which is faster than `d2` draws.

The cache key holds the diagram language as well as the source, because the
same text is one diagram in D2 and another in Mermaid.

### When a Mermaid cell is drawn

A Mermaid cell is drawn exactly when a D2 cell is, as the record on diagram
cells specifies: when the card is shown, and again as its source is edited.
The earlier draft of that record forbade drawing while the author types,
because Mermaid was expected to need a headless browser. The native renderer
draws in milliseconds, so that restriction is withdrawn for both languages.

### Delivery and security

- The drawing MUST reach the page as a `data:` URI image and MUST NOT be
  inserted as markup. An image runs no script and follows no link, so nothing
  in diagram source reaches the page as code.
- Drawing MUST NOT require a trusted workspace. The process gets the fence
  body and nothing else.
- A `click` directive or a link in a diagram is written into the SVG as a
  link. Inside an image it does nothing.

### Configuration inside the fence

Mermaid lets a diagram configure itself, with an `%%{init: …}%%` directive or
a frontmatter block at the top of its body.

- Configuration inside the fence is honoured. A diagram that asks for the
  `dark` theme is drawn in the dark theme.
- It cannot widen what the renderer may do. The resource profile, the
  timeout, and the absence of network access are set on the command line, and
  the image delivery is the implementation's.

### Theme

A drawing sits on a white surface in every editor theme, as a D2 drawing
does. Mermaid ships a dark theme and D2 ships one too, so drawing each diagram
for the editor's theme is possible for both, and remains the open question it
was for D2.

### The degraded state

A Mermaid cell that is not drawn shows its source, labelled, with the reason.
The wording is the same as for D2, with the engine's name in it.

| Cause | What the reader sees |
| --- | --- |
| No renderer | The source, and that no Mermaid renderer was found |
| A syntax error | The renderer's own one-line message |
| A timeout | That the render exceeded 2 seconds |
| A source over 64 KiB | That the source exceeds 64 KiB |

In the preview, a cell that drew before keeps its last drawing, marked stale,
with the reason under it. On a built page there is no earlier drawing, and the
page shows the source.

### Where the renderer comes from

- The toolchain repository pins `merman-cli` beside `d2`, one asset per
  platform with its digest, and each archive carries it at `bin/merman-cli`
  (`bin/merman-cli.exe` on Windows).
- The extension's package for each platform carries the binary at
  `toolchain/bin/`. The extension downloads nothing.
- The extension looks for the renderer in this order: the path in the
  setting `hyperMarkdown.diagram.mermaidPath`, then the bundled binary, then
  `merman-cli` on `PATH`. A setting that names something that is not an
  executable is reported, and the search stops there.
- The site build finds `merman-cli` on `PATH`. Continuous integration installs
  the pinned toolchain and checks `merman-cli --version` beside `d2 --version`.

### The cover's live editor

The cover's live editor shows a diagram as its text, D2 included. Mermaid gets
the same treatment.

## Rationale

- **A native renderer, not Mermaid's own command-line tool.** Mermaid's tool
  is faithful, because it is Mermaid. It needs Node and a pinned Chromium,
  which is hundreds of megabytes in each platform package, and a cold start
  that does not fit in 2 seconds.
- **A native renderer, not mermaid.js inside the preview.** Running the
  library in the webview puts diagram source into a script context in the
  preview, which the image delivery exists to prevent. It also does nothing
  for the site build, which has no browser.
- **`merman-cli`, not `mmdr`.** `mmdr` is the other native renderer with
  binaries for all five platforms, and its binary is a tenth of the size. Its
  own documentation says its output may not match Mermaid's, it draws fewer
  diagram types, and its last release is five months old. `merman-cli` is
  checked against Mermaid, its output is the same bytes on every run, it has
  resource limits built in, and it released four days before this record.
- **The cost is size.** The `merman-cli` binary is about 66 MB unpacked and
  16 MB to 26 MB compressed, per platform. Each platform package of the
  extension grows by that much.

## Backwards Compatibility

- No card changes. A plain `mermaid` fence was a code block and stays one.
- A `mermaid#show` fence was a code block in every earlier release. It is
  drawn from the release that carries this record.
- The settings, the bounds, and the delivery of D2 are unchanged.

## Security Considerations

- A new program runs on text from a cloned repository. It is a pinned binary
  with a checked digest, it reads standard input only, it is bounded in time
  and in resources, and its output is shown as an image.
- The renderer is given no network access.
- SVG is a scripting context. The drawing is never inserted as markup, so a
  script or an event handler in it cannot run.

## Test Plan

Unit tests MUST include, for Mermaid, each test that exists for D2:

- `mermaid#show` is a diagram cell; `mermaid`, `Mermaid#show`, and every other
  kind are not.
- The body of a Mermaid cell yields no link and no edge.
- The cache key differs between the two languages for the same source.
- A missing renderer, a failed render, and an oversized source each show the
  labelled source with the reason.
- A hostile SVG reaches the page as base64 in an image and never as markup.
- The renderer is started with the arguments above.

Tests against the real binary MUST include:

- A flowchart draws with its labels as SVG text, with no `foreignObject`
  element and no `script` element.
- The same source draws the same bytes twice.
- A syntax error comes back as a one-line reason.

Integration tests MUST include:

- A site build of a card with a `mermaid#show` cell and a plain `mermaid`
  fence draws the first and shows the second as code.
- A site build with no renderer shows the source and stays green.

## Open Questions

- Should HQL's `mermaid` format step write `mermaid#show`? Until it does, the
  output of that step is a listing.
- Should a plain `mermaid` fence raise a diagnostic suggesting `#show`? It
  would help a vault brought in from elsewhere, and it would fire on every
  deliberate listing.
- Should a smaller build of the renderer be produced, with only the diagram
  types a documentation site needs? It would cut the package size, and the
  toolchain would then build a binary instead of repackaging a released one.

